    "result": "I've successfully fixed the bug in the dates EOM functionality. Here's a summary of what was accomplished:\n\n## Problem\nThe `end_of_month` function in `invoicing/dates.py` was incorrectly calculating February dates in leap years. It always used 28 days for February regardless of whether the year was a leap year, causing invoices with 'eom' (end of month) payment terms issued in February of leap years to have wrong due dates.\n\n## Solution\nI modified the `end_of_month` function to properly handle leap years by:\n1. Checking if the month is February\n2. Using `calendar.isleap()` to determine if the year is a leap year\n3. Setting February to 29 days for leap years and 28 days for non-leap years\n4. Maintaining the original logic for all other months\n\n## Verification\n- \u2705 The originally failing tests now pass:\n  - `test_end_of_month_leap_february`\n  - `test_eom_terms_in_leap_year`\n- \u2705 All 85 existing tests continue to pass (no regressions)\n- \u2705 The fix correctly handles various leap years (2020, 2024, 2028) and non-leap years (2021, 2022, 2023)\n- \u2705 All other months work correctly\n\nThe fix is general and handles all years correctly, not just 2024, while maintaining full backward compatibility. All tests in the test suite now pass.",
